[
  {
    "file": "web/src/api/client.ts",
    "line": 128,
    "summary": "The SPA's api.mission client object exposes list/get/create/approve/reject but never wires up POST /missions/{id}/requirements or POST /missions/{id}/submit, and MissionDetailScreen.tsx has no UI for either action, even though api/routes/mission.py, services/mission.py, and cli/main.py (mission add-requirement / mission submit) fully support both.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Mission Lead creates a mission via MissionCreateScreen.tsx (whose own copy says 'add requirements and submit for approval once it's created') and lands on MissionDetailScreen.tsx. There is no add-requirement form and no submit button anywhere in that screen, and client.ts has no api.mission.addRequirement/submit methods to call. The mission is permanently stuck in draft via the web app — it can only be submitted by dropping to the CLI (missionctl mission add-requirement / submit) — and execute_mission_transition's MissionHasNoRequirementsError means submit would fail anyway since no requirement can be attached from the SPA."
  },
  {
    "file": "api/routes/assignment.py",
    "line": 65,
    "summary": "Assignment.proposed_at/responded_at (and AuthToken.created_at) are set via models/timestamps.py's utc_now(), a timezone-aware datetime, but SQLAlchemy's default sqlite DATETIME column silently drops the tzinfo on read-back after session.refresh(), so the API returns naive ISO timestamps.",
    "failure_scenario": "POST /assignments/{id}/respond commits and refreshes the assignment, then AssignmentOut serializes responded_at as e.g. '2026-09-16T10:30:00.123456' (no 'Z'/offset), whereas the SPA's own optimistic value in MissionDetailScreen.tsx/MyAssignmentsScreen.tsx uses `new Date().toISOString()` which always includes 'Z'. Verified directly: writing a tz-aware datetime through SQLAlchemy 2.0.52's sqlite dialect and reading it back in this repo's own engine setup returns a naive datetime (tzinfo=None). Any future/consuming code that does `new Date(assignment.proposed_at)` (rather than the current `.slice(0,10)` date-only usage) will interpret the value as local time instead of UTC, silently shifting displayed times by the browser's UTC offset."
